### Differentiating using the power rule

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{d}{dx}\left(x^{-5}\right)
\\\\
&=-5x^{-5-1}\qquad\gray{\text{The power rule}}
\\\\
&=-5x^{-6}
\end{align}$

### Evaluating the derivative

So we found that $\dfrac{d}{dx}\left(\dfrac{1}{x^5}\right)=-5x^{-6}$, which can also be written as $-\dfrac{5}{x^6}$.

Now let's plug $\blueD{x=2}$:

$\begin{align}
-\dfrac{5}{(\blueD{2})^6}&=-\dfrac{5}{64}
\end{align}$
